1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,680
Why did one of his own men shoot

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:06,910
one of the most decorated U-boat commanders of the Second World War?

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:14,110
In the early hours of 14 May 1945, after Germany had already surrendered,

4
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:19,250
a naval captain was killed by the bullet of an 18-year-old German sailor.

5
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:25,430
The officer was Wolfgang Lüth, a U-boat ace who had survived four years of war at sea.

6
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:31,170
Born in 1913 in Riga, then part of the Russian Empire,

7
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,150
he joined the Reichsmarine in April 1933.

8
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:42,350
Between late 1939 and early 1942, Lüth commanded three U-boats.

9
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:48,970
With U-9 he sank the French submarine Doris, the only warship of his career.

10
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:54,480
With U-138 he received the Knight's Cross in October 1940.

11
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:02,950
And with U-43, by the end of 1941, he was among the serving commanders with the most ships sunk.

12
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:10,370
In 1942 he took over U-181, which brought him to the height of his fame.

13
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:16,300
On his first patrol he sank twelve merchant ships and received the Oak Leaves while still at sea.

14
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:24,670
His second patrol lasted more than 200 days without a single port call, one of the longest of the entire war.

15
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:28,140
In April 1943 he received the Swords,

16
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:32,850
and in August, as the first officer of the Kriegsmarine, the Diamonds.

17
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:36,270
In October, Hitler presented them to him in person.

18
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:39,270
But it would be his last patrol.

19
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:41,700
After that, he trained officers.

20
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:48,110
In September 1944 he became commandant of the Naval Academy at Mürwik in Flensburg.

21
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:54,810
It was there, eight months later, that Dönitz, as Hitler's successor, set up the last government of the Reich.

22
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:01,490
After the surrender, the British allowed Lüth to keep armed sentries guarding the academy.

23
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:06,470
He himself had ordered them to shoot anyone who did not give the password.

24
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:10,620
On the night of 14 May, Lüth was walking to his quarters.

25
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:18,010
The sentry, Able Seaman Mathias Gottlob, saw a figure in the darkness but did not recognise it.

26
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:24,910
According to the sentries, Lüth stopped at the first call to halt, but answered none of the three challenges.

27
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:27,730
Gottlob fired a single shot.

28
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:30,890
He later said he had meant to fire into the air.

29
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:34,920
But the bullet struck Lüth in the forehead, killing him instantly.

30
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:40,220
His biographer Jordan Vause thinks it likely that Lüth did answer,

31
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:45,070
and that wind, rain or nerves kept the sentry from hearing him.

32
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:46,710
Whatever the truth:

33
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:49,510
he had survived four years of war at sea,

34
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:54,710
only to die after the surrender from a shot he himself had ordered.
